  <static seed="125" source="Library/Michigan/Chap3Sec7/Q21.pg">
    <statement><p>Find the slope of the tangent to the curve
<m>x^{5}+xy+y^{2} = 43</m>  at <m>(1,6)</m>.</p>
<p>The slope is <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/>.</p>
<p><em>(Enter <alert>undef</alert> if the
slope is not defined at this point.)</em></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-{\frac{11}{13}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>Differentiating with respect to <m>x</m> gives
<m>5 x^{4} +  y +  x y' + 2y y'=0</m>
so that
<m>y' = -\frac{5 x^{4} +  y}{ x + 2 y}</m>.</p>
<p>At the point <m>(1,6)</m> the slope is
<m>- \frac{5\cdot 1^{4}+6}{1+2\cdot 6} = -{\frac{11}{13}}</m>.</p>
</solution>
